WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:04.880
Gauge rulers or gauge scales are not

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.720
machine-specific, they are
stitch and row specific.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.000
In a recent Knitclub meeting,
UA knitter mentioned that she had gauge

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.240
rulers, but she didn't know
what machine they were for.

5
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.720
And this just didn't ring true to me,
so we decided to investigate.

6
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.720
Now, I've removed the color from these

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.360
images so we can examine
the rulers themselves.

8
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.600
We've marked 20 stitches and 30
stitches on this swatch.

9
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.120
Notice that the bottom ruler has
the number 30 printed right on it

10
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.640
in a circle so we can cheat here and we
know that this ruler is gauged for 30

11
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.200
stitches, but the top ruler doesn't
tell us how many stitches to measure.

12
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.520
Now, remember, we read the gauge rulers
in this direction,

13
00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.880
so measuring 30 stitches with the bottom
ruler, we get 15.

14
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.760
Now that translates to 15 stitches
to four inches or 10 centimeters.

15
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:13.720
If we read the top ruler at 20
stitches, it also measures 15.

16
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.320
If we use a regular old ruler,

17
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.200
30 stitches measures, eight inches.
And if we do the math,

18
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:27.760
we also get 15 stitches to four
inches or 10 centimeters.

19
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.600
So all three scales,
no matter what color they are,

20
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:38.240
no matter what you call them, are
measuring the same gauge for my swatch.

21
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.160
The takeaway from this experiment is

22
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.680
that no matter what color the rulers
are, the top ruler is gauged for 20

23
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:51.880
stitches and the bottom ruler
is gauged for 30 stitches.

24
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.560
Now, here's another example,
using three different rulers.

25
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.320
Doesn't matter what machine
was used to knit the sample.

26
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.160
Again, notice that two of the three rulers
don't have any kind of indication about

27
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.240
what gauge to you they're
using or what to measure.

28
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:18.000
The swatch is marked at 40 stitches,
20 stitches and 30 stitches.

29
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.160
Now these are common gauges
for these gauge rulers.

30
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.120
Using the bottom ruler,
40 stitches, measures out at approximately

31
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.400
27 stitches to four
inches or 10 centimeters.

32
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.120
The middle ruler measuring against 20

33
00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:45.200
stitches also measures 27 stitches to four
inches or 10 centimeters.

34
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:51.360
And the top ruler at 30
stitches measures the same.

35
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.320
And using a regular old ruler,

36
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.960
40 stitches measures
approximately eight inches.

37
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.160
Doing the math,

38
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.800
this confirms that the gauge
of the swatch, no matter what ruler we

39
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.560
use, is twenty seven stitches
to four inches or 10 centimeters.

40
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.640
So does this convince you that these

41
00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:26.360
rulers are not machine-specific?
They are stitches and rows specific.

42
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.680
Also, have you noticed that other than

43
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.720
the markers on my rulers,
my little cheat sheets,

44
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.000
I haven't mentioned the words
standard, mid or bulky.

45
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.080
I don't know what machine
these swatches were knit with.

46
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.840
And it really doesn't matter.

47
00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.920
What matters is the number of stitches
and rows that are measured.

48
00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.160
If you have a white or purple or

49
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.760
a tangerine color ruler,
just confirm how it's gauged.

50
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.640
Is it gauged at 20,
30, 40 or maybe even some other stitch

51
00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:09.600
count. Once you establish what
that is, mark the ruler.

52
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:16.200
So there's no guesswork and you can
use this ruler again and again.

53
00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.000
One last thing.

54
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.880
In studying my three gauge rulers,

55
00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:26.760
I noticed that the largest number
on the stitch side of the rulers is twice

56
00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.720
the stitch count that
the ruler is gauged to.

57
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.880
In other words, the yellow ruler is a 30

58
00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.880
stitch gauge and the largest
number on that scale is 60.

59
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.680
On the 20 stitch gauge ruler,
its largest number is 40.

60
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.880
On the 40 stitch gauge ruler, the largest
number on the stitch scale is 80.

61
00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.040
Now, this isn't true on the
row side of the scales.

62
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:06.080
Hopefully, this will help you identify any
mystery rulers that you have and also

63
00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:11.240
convince you that the gauge
rulers are not machines specific.

